  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 145 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nIn the wake of Sunday's cold front, much cooler temperatures and dry \nconditions take hold to start the Thanksgiving week. While Monday \nwill certainly feel like a shock to the system after the warm \nweather this past week, high temperatures in the mid-40s to low 50s \nthat settle in will be close to (if not a degree or two below) late \nNovember normals. Weaker winds will also take the sting out of the \ncolder temperatures by then, with the surface high approaching from \nthe north. Deterministic guidance along with a majority of ensemble \nmembers suggest the surface ridge makes a more southward progress by \nTuesday, which may allow for colder temperatures to settle in. NBM \nprobabilities of exceeding 50 degrees areawide are virtually zero \nacross the forecast area, lending confidence that Tuesday should be \nthe coldest day of the week in terms of high temperatures.\n\nBy late Tuesday, ensemble guidance continues to model a broad upper-\nlevel trough tracking through the Desert Southwest into the Southern \nPlains. It is then forecast to interact with a northern stream wave \ndropping through the Plains, leading to a deepening surface cyclone \nWednesday that begins to track to the northeast from the Gulf Coast. \nGiven the colder air in place, some low threat for a rain/snow mix \ncontinues to exist in the guidance associated with this storm. The \nexact track of this system is very dependent on how these two waves \ninteract, the details of which vary quite a bit in ensemble \nguidance. That said, there seems to be a decided trend towards a \nlater strengthening of the system further east of our forecast area. \nEven if the few solutions that show an earlier, western position of \nthe low come true, antecedent warmth and very poor snow-liquid \nratios would preclude any winter weather threat. This decreasing \nthreat is further supported by a steady decreasing trend in the \nprobability of 0.1 inches of snow in the NBM across the region. As \nsuch, the busy travel day (and pretty much the entire Thanksgiving \nholiday weekend) should stay dry and seasonable.",
